<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:13:14Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>How climate change and food insecurity could fuel future conflicts</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e31380a422208f75e891baa1c3061c9322499de1dd9686ee8f3abb65398110d9</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:13:08Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T07:47:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431--8e63f64c4115.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Climate change, conflict, and trade tensions are putting pressure on the global food system. New research suggests the biggest risk is not food shortages, but whether societies can peacefully adapt to change.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Ebola: Why children are last to be treated in medical crises</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:661f0991b3eecf5b8362f9d7b219ad8259cbd0b590765614a1b8751efdddd56c</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:13:14Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T06:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ebola-why-children-are-last-to-be-treated-in-medical-crises/a-79050107--c059b44b485e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ebola-why-children-are-last-to-be-treated-in-medical-crises/a-79050107?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">In the latest Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo, children are more likely to die than adults. But it's not always due to the disease. Specific medicines for younger people are often harder to come by.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisia: Judge orders arrest of journalist Mohamed Yousfi</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:865c6ae1af87f75f769288831a28cbf578f47ac4d5555aee77aebb05a559f89a</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:12:13Z</updated><published>2026-09-05T03:39:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/tunisia-judge-orders-arrest-of-journalist-mohamed-yousfi/a-79040802--a89422e15909.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/tunisia-judge-orders-arrest-of-journalist-mohamed-yousfi/a-79040802?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A journalist and vocal critic of the Tunisian president, Mohamed Yousfi has been taken into custody ahead of an interview. A union called the arrest an attack on all journalists' rights.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>UN approves world map reflecting true size of Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d28a5c7eb3f50dcf135a0c42a7432f2fc524dff3682508c265030567b9679494</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:12:13Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T23:34:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/un-approves-world-map-reflecting-true-size-of-africa/a-79040629--e6486bc869d9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/un-approves-world-map-reflecting-true-size-of-africa/a-79040629?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The UN General Assembly has voted to adopt a map that more accurately shows the size of the continents. Only the United States voted against the measure, which projects the country as smaller than on traditional maps.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria: Suspected oil thieves dead after inhaling fumes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3617e45a9cf0b57fa1d78b5bc610138ab630955d0e487df7b442b084ec62718a</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:12:13Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T15:35:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nigeria-suspected-oil-thieves-dead-after-inhaling-fumes/a-79001997--76c34d7b815f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nigeria-suspected-oil-thieves-dead-after-inhaling-fumes/a-79001997?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A Nigerian environmental group says that at least 37 people died trying to siphon crude oil off a ship illegally. Police acknowledged a fatal incident but gave fewer details, saying investigations continued.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisia's activists decry civil society crackdown</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:45b2aa639ae75339b259812c6a3d61b0271114ccefb8a98c03c0366d76e07db4</id><updated>2026-09-06T12:12:13Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T04:01:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/tunisia-s-activists-decry-civil-society-crackdown/a-78614966--e657463dce69.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/tunisia-s-activists-decry-civil-society-crackdown/a-78614966?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Five years into Tunisian President Kais Saied’s increasingly authoritarian rule, rights groups warn that the country’s once vibrant civil society is being silenced.</summary><category term="TOP STORIES" /></entry></feed>
